{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012AJwXoHP9rb2UATQN64LM9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UV8vdWoyvMWQvY1i8sYMCV","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **methods** and **experiments**. \n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\nCoRL evaluates papers on: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance (to robot learning). \nScoring: 1-10 scale (10=top 5% seminal, 8=clear accept, 6=marginally above threshold, 5=marginally below, 3=clear reject).\nConfidence: 1-5 (5=absolute certainty, 3=fairly confident).\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Where Do Action-Relevant Representations Come From in Vision-Language-Action Models? A Representational Analysis of OpenVLA\"\n\nThe paper performs RSA (Representational Similarity Analysis) on OpenVLA, decomposing contributions of the frozen SigLIP vision encoder, the learned MLP projector, and the Llama-2 LLM backbone. Using a 2×2 factorial design (trained/untrained × with/without instruction) on two LIBERO benchmarks:\n\n**Three claims:**\n1. Vision encoder dominance: SigLIP+projector achieves RSA ρ=0.615 with action structure, far exceeding full VLA (ρ≈0.488) and DINOv2 (ρ=0.388).\n2. LLM does not enhance action-relevant structure: Passing through LLM reduces alignment by Δρ≈0.13. Per-layer analysis shows Spearman ρ=−0.62 (non-monotonic, bump at layers 12-16).\n3. Training and instruction invariance: 2×2 matrix collapses to narrow band ρ∈[0.480, 0.489], bootstrap CIs include zero. Instruction effect (Δ≈0.02) is 1/5 of architecture effect (Δ≈0.13).\n\n**Experiments:**\n- Table 1: Core 2×2 RSA comparison (6 conditions on LIBERO-Goal)\n- Table 2: Instruction ablation with 3 random controls (random words, wrong-task, fixed string)\n- Table 3-4: CKA verification (Kendall τ=0.87 with RSA ordering)\n- Table 5: LIBERO-Spatial cross-benchmark (qualitatively identical results, absolute RSA ~0.03 lower)\n- Appendix A: Per-layer RSA curve (32 layers + projector)\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes these imagined experiments with expected results:\n\n1. **Per-layer RSA degradation curve**: Spearman ρ(layer, RSA)=-0.62, 8 monotonicity violations, bump at L12-16 (magnitude 0.06), overall drop 0.615→0.47.\n\n2. **Random instruction control**: Real instruction RSA=0.488, random controls mean=0.467, Δ=0.021. Architecture effect=0.127 for comparison. Random instruction RSA *lower* than no-instruction baseline (noise injection effect).\n\n3. **CKA verification**: CKA ordering agrees with RSA (τ=0.87) but SigLIP-VLA gap shrinks (0.06 vs 0.127). 4 per-layer ordering reversals at layers 7,15,22,29 (max magnitude 0.03). Training effect CKA CI [-0.04, 0.07] includes zero.\n\n4. **LIBERO-Spatial extension**: All orderings preserved, absolute values ~0.03 lower. SigLIP-VLA gap 0.12 vs 0.127.\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n### 1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### 2. Strengths (bullet points)\nFocus on: experimental design rigor, statistical methodology, controls, reproducibility.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses (bullet points)\nFocus on: \n- Methodological gaps or concerns\n- Missing experiments or controls\n- Statistical rigor issues\n- Generalizability concerns\n- Whether RSA is the right/only metric for \"action-relevant structure\"\n- The gap between representational analysis and behavioral/functional conclusions\n- Single architecture (OpenVLA only)\n- LIBERO-only evaluation (simulation, single robot)\n- Whether the non-monotonic per-layer pattern undermines the \"LLM disrupts\" claim\n- Whether the projector is doing the heavy lifting (it's learned, not frozen)\n- Sample size / number of observations for RSA computation\n\n### 4. Questions to Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n\n### 5. Preliminary Scores\n- Overall: [1-10]\n- Confidence: [1-5]\n- Justification for score (2-3 sentences)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a representational analysis paper, not a new method paper. Evaluate it on the strength of its scientific claims and experimental evidence."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":734,"cache_read_input_tokens":59260,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":734},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fa2e3739-cf35-4bf4-84d7-4c529d34ab0f","uuid":"caef5318-0988-4c7f-861f-99ffd7da4272"}
