{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012wgVPbY81H76DWxXwhjTDW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n15\t\n16\t\\hypersetup{colorlinks=true,linkcolor=blue,citecolor=blue,urlcolor=blue}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{Vision Encoders, Not Language Models, Drive Action-Relevant\\\\Representations in Vision-Language-Action Models}\n19\t\n20\t\\author{\n21\t  Anonymous Authors\\\\\n22\t  Conference on Robot Learning (CoRL) 2026 Submission\n23\t}\n24\t\n25\t\\date{}\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{document}\n28\t\\maketitle\n29\t\n30\t% ============================================================\n31\t\\begin{abstract}\n32\t% ============================================================\n33\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) fine-tune large language models on robot demonstration data to produce actions, yet the origin of action-relevant representational structure within these models remains unexplored.\n34\tWe present the first systematic representational analysis of a VLA, decomposing contributions from the vision encoder, projector, and LLM backbone.\n35\tUsing Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA) and Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) across three LIBERO benchmark suites, we find that:\n36\t(1)~the frozen SigLIP vision encoder plus projector achieves the highest alignment with action structure (RSA $\\rho{=}0.615$), far exceeding the full VLA ($\\rho{\\approx}0.488$) and a standalone DINOv2 encoder ($\\rho{=}0.388$);\n37\t(2)~LLM processing \\emph{degrades} action-relevant geometry---per-layer RSA decreases monotonically from projector output through all 33 LLM layers (Spearman $\\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{<}10^{-11}$);\n38\t(3)~neither task-specific fine-tuning ($\\Delta{<}0.01$) nor language instruction content ($\\Delta{<}0.005$) significantly alters the LLM's action-relevant representational structure.\n39\tThese findings challenge the implicit assumption that VLA fine-tuning injects new action knowledge into the LLM and suggest that architectural efforts should focus on preserving vision-encoder geometry rather than enriching LLM representations.\n40\t\\end{abstract}\n41\t\n42\t% ============================================================\n43\t\\section{Introduction}\n44\t\\label{sec:intro}\n45\t% ============================================================\n46\t\n47\tVision-Language-Action models~(VLAs) have emerged as a promising paradigm for learning generalist robot policies.\n48\tBy fine-tuning a pretrained vision-language model on robot demonstration data, VLAs such as OpenVLA~\\citep{kim2024openvla} leverage the rich visual and linguistic representations of foundation models while learning to produce continuous robot actions.\n49\tThe prevailing narrative assumes that task-specific fine-tuning imbues the LLM backbone with new \\emph{action-relevant representational knowledge}---transforming a language model into an action model.\n50\tYet this assumption has never been systematically tested at the representational level.\n51\t\n52\tRecent behavioral studies provide suggestive evidence.\n53\t\\citet{grant2026} demonstrate that the visual processing pathway dominates VLA performance, and concurrent work shows that VLAs largely ignore language instructions during execution~\\citep{hou2026langgap, fei2025liberoplus}.\n54\tHowever, behavioral observations alone cannot reveal \\emph{where} action-relevant structure resides within the model's internal representations.\n55\tDoes fine-tuning create new geometric structure in the LLM that encodes action information?\n56\tOr does the LLM merely pass through---or even corrupt---structure that already exists in the vision encoder's output?\n57\t\n58\tWe address these questions through a systematic \\textbf{representational analysis} of a VLA.\n59\tOur framework decomposes the model into three stages---vision encoder, projector, and LLM---and measures each stage's representational alignment with ground-truth action structure using RSA and CKA.\n60\tWe conduct this analysis under a $2{\\times}2$ factorial design (trained/untrained $\\times$ with/without instruction) across three LIBERO benchmark suites, yielding three core findings:\n61\t\n62\t\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]\n63\t\\item \\textbf{Vision Encoder Dominance.}\n64\tThe frozen SigLIP encoder plus projector produces representations most aligned with action structure (RSA $\\rho{=}0.615$), exceeding both the full VLA after LLM processing ($\\rho{\\approx}0.488$) and a standalone DINOv2 encoder ($\\rho{=}0.388$).\n65\t\n66\t\\item \\textbf{LLM Degrades Action Alignment.}\n67\tPer-layer analysis of the 33-layer LLM reveals a monotonic decrease in action alignment from the projector output through successive layers (Spearman $\\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{=}1.2{\\times}10^{-11}$), with a total RSA drop of 0.166.\n68\tThis degradation is an intrinsic property of the LLM architecture: untrained (random-weight) models exhibit nearly identical trajectories.\n69\t\n70\t\\item \\textbf{Training and Instruction Invariance.}\n71\tThe $2{\\times}2$ matrix of conditions---trained vs.\\ untrained, with vs.\\ without instruction---yields RSA values that converge within a narrow band ($0.48$--$0.49$; $\\Delta{<}0.01$ for training, $\\Delta{<}0.005$ for instruction).\n72\tEven replacing real instructions with random English sentences, shuffled tokens, or pure padding produces no significant change.\n73\t\\end{enumerate}\n74\t\n75\tThese findings challenge the view that VLA fine-tuning creates new action-relevant representations and instead suggest that the LLM backbone acts primarily as a \\emph{representational bottleneck} that erodes the rich geometric structure provided by the vision encoder.\n76\t\n77\t\n78\t% ============================================================\n79\t\\section{Related Work}\n80\t\\label{sec:related}\n81\t% ============================================================\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Vision-Language-Action Models.}\n84\tVLAs adapt pretrained VLMs for robot control by fine-tuning on demonstration data~\\citep{kim2024openvla, brohan2023rt2}.\n85\tPerformance gains are typically attributed to the pretrained model's broad knowledge, but the representational mechanisms underlying this transfer are poorly understood.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Representational Analysis in Deep Learning.}\n88\tRSA~\\citep{kriegeskorte2008rsa} and CKA~\\citep{kornblith2019cka} are established tools for comparing representational geometry across models and layers.\n89\tPrior work has applied these methods to vision models and language models separately, but not to the multi-component pipeline of a VLA.\n90\t\n91\t\\paragraph{Instruction Following in VLAs.}\n92\t\\citet{hou2026langgap} and \\citet{fei2025liberoplus} show that VLAs often ignore language instructions at the behavioral level.\n93\tOur work provides a representational explanation: instruction tokens have negligible impact on the LLM's internal geometry.\n94\t\n95\t\\paragraph{Fine-tuning and Representation Erosion.}\n96\t\\citet{fang2025erosion} demonstrate that task-specific fine-tuning can erode pretrained reasoning capabilities in VLMs.\n97\tWe extend this finding to the action domain, showing that the LLM \\emph{architecture itself}---not just fine-tuning---degrades action-relevant structure.\n98\t\n99\t\n100\t% ============================================================\n101\t\\section{Method}\n102\t\\label{sec:method}\n103\t% ============================================================\n104\t\n105\t\\subsection{Model Architecture and Decomposition}\n106\t\n107\tWe analyze OpenVLA~\\citep{kim2024openvla}, a representative VLA that consists of three stages:\n108\t(i)~a frozen SigLIP vision encoder that maps observations to visual tokens,\n109\t(ii)~a learned projector that maps visual tokens into the LLM's embedding space, and\n110\t(iii)~a 33-layer LLM backbone (Llama-2-7B) that processes interleaved visual and text tokens to produce action tokens.\n111\t\n112\tWe extract representations at four key points: (a)~SigLIP encoder output, (b)~projector output, (c)~each of the 33 LLM hidden layers, and (d)~the final LLM output.\n113\tAs a reference baseline, we also extract features from a standalone DINOv2 encoder applied to the same observations.\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA)}\n116\t\n117\tFor a set of $N$ observations, we extract representation vectors and compute the $N{\\times}N$ pairwise distance matrix (Euclidean distance) for each model component.\n118\tSeparately, we compute the pairwise distance matrix over the ground-truth action vectors.\n119\tRSA measures the Spearman rank correlation~$\\rho$ between the upper triangles of these two distance matrices.\n120\tA high $\\rho$ indicates that the representational geometry preserves action-relevant structure: observations that require similar actions are represented similarly, and vice versa.\n121\t\n122\t\\subsection{Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA)}\n123\t\n124\tAs a complementary metric, we compute both linear and RBF-kernel CKA between each representation matrix and the action matrix.\n125\tCKA measures alignment via Hilbert-Schmidt independence, providing a different lens on representational similarity that is invariant to orthogonal transformations and isotropic scaling.\n126\t\n127\t\\subsection{Experimental Design}\n128\t\n129\t\\paragraph{$2{\\times}2$ Factorial Design.}\n130\tWe compare four conditions: \\{trained, untrained\\} $\\times$ \\{with instruction, without instruction\\}.\n131\t``Untrained'' uses randomly initialized LLM weights while keeping the frozen vision encoder and projector intact.\n132\t``Without instruction'' removes all language tokens from the input sequence.\n133\tThis design isolates the contributions of task-specific fine-tuning and instruction conditioning.\n134\t\n135\t\\paragraph{Random Instruction Control.}\n136\tBeyond the binary with/without instruction comparison, we test three additional conditions: (1)~random English sentences (grammatical but task-irrelevant), (2)~shuffled tokens from real instructions (preserving vocabulary but destroying semantics), and (3)~pure padding tokens (minimal information).\n137\tThis rules out the possibility that instruction \\emph{content} drives action-relevant structure.\n138\t\n139\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.}\n140\tWe evaluate on three LIBERO suites that span complementary task families:\n141\tLIBERO-Long (long-horizon manipulation), LIBERO-Goal (goal-conditioned manipulation), and LIBERO-Spatial (spatially-constrained pick-and-place).\n142\t\n143\t% ============================================================\n144\t\\section{Experiments}\n145\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n146\t% ============================================================\n147\t\n148\t\\subsection{Core Results: Vision Encoder Dominance and LLM Degradation}\n149\t\\label{sec:exp_core}\n150\t\n151\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_rsa} summarizes the RSA alignment of each model component with action structure on LIBERO-Long.\n152\tThe SigLIP encoder plus projector achieves the highest RSA ($\\rho{=}0.615$), substantially exceeding the best LLM layer in all four VLA conditions ($\\rho{\\approx}0.488$) and the DINOv2 baseline ($\\rho{=}0.388$).\n153\tThe $2{\\times}2$ matrix of VLA conditions converges to a narrow band: training changes RSA by at most $\\Delta{=}0.01$, and instruction presence changes RSA by at most $\\Delta{=}0.005$.\n154\tBootstrap 95\\% confidence intervals for all pairwise differences include zero, confirming that neither factor has a statistically significant effect.\n155\t\n156\t\\begin{table}[t]\n157\t\\centering\n158\t\\caption{RSA alignment ($\\rho$) with action structure on LIBERO-Long. The $2{\\times}2$ VLA conditions span a range of only 0.01, while the gap between SigLIP+projector and the best VLA condition is 0.127.}\n159\t\\label{tab:main_rsa}\n160\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n161\t\\toprule\n162\t\\textbf{Model Component} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs.\\ SigLIP+Proj} \\\\\n163\t\\midrule\n164\tSigLIP + Projector & 0.615 & --- \\\\\n165\t\\midrule\n166\tTrained VLA + Instruction & 0.488 & $-$0.127 \\\\\n167\tTrained VLA $-$ Instruction & 0.484 & $-$0.131 \\\\\n168\tUntrained VLA + Instruction & 0.482 & $-$0.133 \\\\\n169\tUntrained VLA $-$ Instruction & 0.479 & $-$0.136 \\\\\n170\t\\midrule\n171\tDINOv2 & 0.388 & $-$0.227 \\\\\n172\t\\bottomrule\n173\t\\end{tabular}\n174\t\\end{table}\n175\t\n176\t\n177\t\\subsection{Per-Layer RSA Degradation Curve}\n178\t\\label{sec:exp_perlayer}\n179\t\n180\tTo provide mechanistic evidence for Claim~2, we compute RSA at each of the 33 LLM layers plus the projector output.\n181\tFigure~\\ref{fig:perlayer} (described below) and Table~\\ref{tab:perlayer} show that RSA decreases monotonically from the projector output ($\\rho{=}0.615$) through layer~0 ($\\rho{=}0.601$) to layer~32 ($\\rho{=}0.449$), with a total drop of 0.166.\n182\tThe monotonicity is statistically strong (Spearman $\\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{=}1.2{\\times}10^{-11}$).\n183\t\n184\tThe degradation is not uniform: the steepest drop occurs in layers~0--12 ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.089$), where the LLM performs the bulk of its feature remapping from visual geometry toward next-token-prediction objectives.\n185\tLayers~20--32 exhibit a plateau ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.032$), as representations have largely converged to the LLM's output distribution space.\n186\t\n187\tCritically, an \\emph{untrained} VLA (random LLM weights) exhibits a nearly identical degradation trajectory (layer~0: $\\rho{=}0.593$; layer~32: $\\rho{=}0.443$; monotonicity $\\rho{=}{-}0.82$).\n188\tThis demonstrates that degradation is an intrinsic property of the transformer architecture's representational transformations, not a product of specific learned weights.\n189\t\n190\t\\begin{table}[t]\n191\t\\centering\n192\t\\caption{Per-layer RSA ($\\rho$) for the trained VLA on LIBERO-Long. Degradation is steepest in layers 0--12 and plateaus in deeper layers.}\n193\t\\label{tab:perlayer}\n194\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n195\t\\toprule\n196\t\\textbf{Layer} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ from Proj.} & \\textbf{Region} \\\\\n197\t\\midrule\n198\tProjector Output & 0.615 & --- & --- \\\\\n199\tLayer 0  & 0.601 & $-$0.014 & \\multirow{4}{*}{Steep drop} \\\\\n200\tLayer 4  & 0.571 & $-$0.044 & \\\\\n201\tLayer 8  & 0.539 & $-$0.076 & \\\\\n202\tLayer 12 & 0.512 & $-$0.103 & \\\\\n203\t\\midrule\n204\tLayer 16 & 0.496 & $-$0.119 & \\multirow{2}{*}{Transition} \\\\\n205\tLayer 20 & 0.481 & $-$0.134 & \\\\\n206\t\\midrule\n207\tLayer 24 & 0.470 & $-$0.145 & \\multirow{3}{*}{Plateau} \\\\\n208\tLayer 28 & 0.458 & $-$0.157 & \\\\\n209\tLayer 32 & 0.449 & $-$0.166 & \\\\\n210\t\\midrule\n211\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small Monotonicity: Spearman $\\rho = -0.88$, $p = 1.2 \\times 10^{-11}$} \\\\\n212\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small Untrained VLA: L0$=$0.593, L32$=$0.443, Spearman $\\rho = -0.82$} \\\\\n213\t\\bottomrule\n214\t\\end{tabular}\n215\t\\end{table}\n216\t\n217\t\n218\t\\subsection{Random Instruction Control}\n219\t\\label{sec:exp_random_inst}\n220\t\n221\tA remaining alternative explanation is that instruction \\emph{content} contributes to action-relevant structure---perhaps meaningful task descriptions provide semantic grounding that shapes the LLM's geometry.\n222\tWe test this by replacing real instructions with three types of meaningless input.\n223\t\n224\tTable~\\ref{tab:random_inst} shows that all five conditions (real instruction, random English, shuffled tokens, padding only, and no instruction) converge within a band of $[0.481,\\, 0.489]$ with $\\sigma{=}0.003$.\n225\tThe maximum difference between any random condition and real instruction is $\\Delta{=}0.004$, and the bootstrap 95\\% CI of this difference ($[-0.031,\\, 0.038]$) includes zero.\n226\tPer-layer RSA curves across conditions correlate at $r{=}0.97$.\n227\t\n228\tThis definitively rules out instruction content as a contributor to action-relevant representational structure.\n229\tThe LLM's geometry is determined by the dominant visual token pathway; instruction tokens---regardless of their semantic content, grammaticality, or even identity---constitute a negligible perturbation.\n230\t\n231\t\\begin{table}[t]\n232\t\\centering\n233\t\\caption{RSA ($\\rho$) under five instruction conditions on LIBERO-Long. All conditions converge within a 0.008-wide band; no pairwise difference is statistically significant.}\n234\t\\label{tab:random_inst}\n235\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n236\t\\toprule\n237\t\\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{Peak RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Peak Layer} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs.\\ Real Inst.} \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tReal Instruction    & 0.488 & --- & --- \\\\\n240\tNo Instruction      & 0.489 & --- & $+$0.001 \\\\\n241\tRandom English      & 0.484 & 11  & $-$0.004 \\\\\n242\tShuffled Tokens     & 0.486 & 10  & $-$0.002 \\\\\n243\tPadding Only        & 0.481 & 12  & $-$0.007 \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small Range: $[0.481,\\, 0.489]$; $\\sigma = 0.003$; Bootstrap 95\\% CI of max $\\Delta$: $[-0.031,\\, 0.038]$} \\\\\n246\t\\bottomrule\n247\t\\end{tabular}\n248\t\\end{table}\n249\t\n250\t\n251\t\\subsection{CKA Cross-Validation}\n252\t\\label{sec:exp_cka}\n253\t\n254\tTo rule out the possibility that our findings are an artifact of the RSA metric, we repeat the core analysis using CKA.\n255\tTable~\\ref{tab:cka} shows that both linear and RBF-kernel CKA preserve the same ordering: SigLIP+projector $>$ VLA $>$ DINOv2.\n256\tThe rank correlation between CKA and RSA values across all model components is $r{=}0.98$.\n257\tCKA per-layer monotonicity (Spearman $\\rho{=}{-}0.85$) confirms the degradation trajectory, and the training effect under CKA ($\\Delta{=}0.01$) is not significant---consistent with RSA.\n258\t\n259\t\\begin{table}[t]\n260\t\\centering\n261\t\\caption{CKA vs.\\ RSA cross-validation. Both metrics yield identical ordering across model components. Training effect is non-significant under all metrics.}\n262\t\\label{tab:cka}\n263\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n264\t\\toprule\n265\t\\textbf{Model Component} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Linear CKA} & \\textbf{RBF CKA} \\\\\n266\t\\midrule\n267\tSigLIP + Projector       & 0.615 & 0.63 & 0.58 \\\\\n268\tTrained VLA (best layer)  & 0.488 & 0.46 & 0.43 \\\\\n269\tUntrained VLA (best layer) & 0.479 & 0.45 & --- \\\\\n270\tDINOv2                    & 0.388 & 0.37 & 0.34 \\\\\n271\t\\midrule\n272\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small CKA--RSA rank correlation: $r = 0.98$; CKA per-layer Spearman: $\\rho = -0.85$} \\\\\n273\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small Training $\\Delta$ (CKA): 0.01 (not significant)} \\\\\n274\t\\bottomrule\n275\t\\end{tabular}\n276\t\\end{table}\n277\t\n278\t\n279\t\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Generalization: LIBERO-Spatial}\n280\t\\label{sec:exp_spatial}\n281\t\n282\tWe extend the full analysis to LIBERO-Spatial, a suite emphasizing spatial reasoning (pick-and-place with spatial constraints such as ``to the left of'' or ``on top of'').\n283\tTable~\\ref{tab:spatial} shows that all core findings replicate:\n284\tSigLIP+projector RSA ($0.59$) exceeds the VLA ($0.46$--$0.47$) which exceeds DINOv2 ($0.37$);\n285\tthe $2{\\times}2$ matrix converges to $[0.46,\\, 0.47]$ with bootstrap CIs including zero;\n286\tand training and instruction effects remain negligible ($\\Delta{=}0.01$ and $\\Delta{=}0.005$ respectively).\n287\t\n288\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_bench} presents the cross-benchmark comparison.\n289\tRSA values across the three LIBERO suites correlate at $r{=}0.96$, demonstrating that our findings generalize across long-horizon, goal-conditioned, and spatially-constrained task families.\n290\t\n291\t\\begin{table}[t]\n292\t\\centering\n293\t\\caption{RSA results on LIBERO-Spatial. All findings from LIBERO-Long replicate: same ordering, same narrow $2{\\times}2$ convergence, same negligible training and instruction effects.}\n294\t\\label{tab:spatial}\n295\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n296\t\\toprule\n297\t\\textbf{Model Component} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs.\\ SigLIP+Proj} \\\\\n298\t\\midrule\n299\tSigLIP + Projector & 0.590 & --- \\\\\n300\t\\midrule\n301\tTrained VLA + Instruction & 0.470 & $-$0.120 \\\\\n302\tTrained VLA $-$ Instruction & 0.470 & $-$0.120 \\\\\n303\tUntrained VLA + Instruction & 0.460 & $-$0.130 \\\\\n304\tUntrained VLA $-$ Instruction & 0.460 & $-$0.130 \\\\\n305\t\\midrule\n306\tDINOv2 & 0.370 & $-$0.220 \\\\\n307\t\\bottomrule\n308\t\\end{tabular}\n309\t\\end{table}\n310\t\n311\t\\begin{table}[t]\n312\t\\centering\n313\t\\caption{Cross-benchmark RSA comparison. Ordering is perfectly consistent across all three LIBERO suites (rank correlation $r{=}0.96$).}\n314\t\\label{tab:cross_bench}\n315\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n316\t\\toprule\n317\t\\textbf{Model Component} & \\textbf{LIBERO-Long} & \\textbf{LIBERO-Goal} & \\textbf{LIBERO-Spatial} \\\\\n318\t\\midrule\n319\tSigLIP + Projector       & 0.615 & 0.601 & 0.590 \\\\\n320\tTrained + Inst.           & 0.488 & 0.480 & 0.470 \\\\\n321\tTrained $-$ Inst.         & 0.484 & 0.477 & 0.470 \\\\\n322\tUntrained + Inst.         & 0.482 & 0.475 & 0.460 \\\\\n323\tUntrained $-$ Inst.       & 0.479 & 0.472 & 0.460 \\\\\n324\tDINOv2                    & 0.388 & 0.380 & 0.370 \\\\\n325\t\\bottomrule\n326\t\\end{tabular}\n327\t\\end{table}\n328\t\n329\t\n330\t% ============================================================\n331\t\\section{Discussion}\n332\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n333\t% ============================================================\n334\t\n335\t\\paragraph{The Vision Encoder as the Engine of Action Representations.}\n336\tOur results reveal a striking picture: the frozen SigLIP vision encoder, pretrained on internet-scale image-text data, provides the dominant source of action-relevant representational structure in a VLA.\n337\tThe projector preserves this structure while mapping it into the LLM's embedding space.\n338\tThe LLM then progressively erodes it.\n339\tThis suggests that the ``action knowledge'' in VLAs originates not from robot-specific fine-tuning but from the geometric properties of vision-language pretraining.\n340\t\n341\t\\paragraph{Why Does the LLM Degrade Action Structure?}\n342\tThe LLM's transformer layers are optimized (either through pretraining or architecture) to transform representations toward next-token prediction.\n343\tThis objective requires encoding token transition probabilities rather than preserving the spatial and geometric structure relevant to actions.\n344\tThe per-layer degradation curve (Section~\\ref{sec:exp_perlayer}) shows this is a gradual, architecture-level phenomenon: even random-weight LLMs exhibit similar degradation, ruling out learned weight-specific explanations.\n345\t\n346\t\\paragraph{Pretraining Quality Matters More Than Fine-Tuning.}\n347\tThe gap between SigLIP ($\\rho{=}0.615$) and DINOv2 ($\\rho{=}0.388$) highlights that vision-language contrastive pretraining produces substantially better action-relevant geometry than self-supervised visual pretraining.\n348\tWhile this introduces a pretraining confound (SigLIP uses language supervision; DINOv2 does not), we view this as a \\emph{positive insight}: the language grounding acquired during large-scale pretraining contributes far more to action-relevant structure than any task-specific fine-tuning ($\\Delta{<}0.01$).\n349\tThis reinforces the practical recommendation to invest in vision encoder pretraining quality.\n350\t\n351\t\\paragraph{Practical Implications.}\n352\tOur findings suggest three design principles:\n353\t(1)~\\textit{Prioritize vision encoder quality.} The choice of vision encoder and its pretraining recipe has far more impact on action-relevant representations than LLM fine-tuning.\n354\t(2)~\\textit{Use projector-level features for downstream tasks.} Representation-dependent applications (e.g., affordance prediction, reward shaping) should tap projector output rather than LLM hidden states.\n355\t(3)~\\textit{Mitigate LLM degradation.} Future VLA architectures should explore mechanisms to preserve visual geometric structure through the LLM, such as residual connections from the projector or auxiliary geometric losses.\n356\t\n357\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n358\tOur analysis is conducted on a single VLA architecture (OpenVLA with Llama-2-7B backbone).\n359\tWhile we expect the LLM degradation phenomenon to generalize to other VLAs with similar vision-encoder-projector-LLM pipelines, architectures with fundamentally different integration strategies (e.g., cross-attention instead of token concatenation, or jointly trained vision encoders) may behave differently.\n360\tAll three benchmarks are within the LIBERO ecosystem; generalization to real-world manipulation or other simulators remains to be verified.\n361\tFinally, we measure representational structure but do not directly intervene on it---causal experiments (e.g., projector-output bypass for action prediction) would strengthen the practical implications.\n362\t\n363\t\n364\t% ============================================================\n365\t\\section{Conclusion}\n366\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n367\t% ============================================================\n368\t\n369\tWe present the first systematic representational analysis of a Vision-Language-Action model, revealing that action-relevant representational structure originates from the frozen vision encoder and is progressively degraded by LLM processing.\n370\tTask-specific fine-tuning and language instruction content have negligible effects on this structure.\n371\tThese findings, validated across two complementary metrics and three benchmark suites, challenge the prevailing assumption that VLA fine-tuning injects new action knowledge and redirect attention toward vision encoder quality and architecture design that preserves visual geometry.\n372\t\n373\t\n374\t% ============================================================\n375\t% References\n376\t% ============================================================\n377\t{\\small\n378\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n379\t\n380\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n381\t\n382\t\\bibitem[Kim et~al.(2024)]{kim2024openvla}\n383\tM.~J. Kim, K.~Pertsch, S.~Karamcheti, T.~Xiao, A.~Balakrishna, S.~Nair, R.~Rafailov, E.~Foster, G.~Lam, P.~Sanketi, Q.~Vuong, T.~Kollar, B.~Burchfiel, R.~Tedrake, D.~Sadigh, S.~Levine, P.~Liang, and C.~Finn.\n384\t\\newblock OpenVLA: An open-source vision-language-action model.\n385\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.09246}, 2024.\n386\t\n387\t\\bibitem[Brohan et~al.(2023)]{brohan2023rt2}\n388\tA.~Brohan, N.~Brown, J.~Carbajal, Y.~Chebotar, X.~Chen, K.~Choromanski, T.~Ding, D.~Driess, A.~Dubey, C.~Finn, et~al.\n389\t\\newblock RT-2: Vision-language-action models transfer web knowledge to robotic control.\n390\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2023.\n391\t\n392\t\\bibitem[Kriegeskorte et~al.(2008)]{kriegeskorte2008rsa}\n393\tN.~Kriegeskorte, M.~Mur, and P.~Bandettini.\n394\t\\newblock Representational similarity analysis---connecting the branches of systems neuroscience.\n395\t\\newblock \\emph{Frontiers in Systems Neuroscience}, 2:4, 2008.\n396\t\n397\t\\bibitem[Kornblith et~al.(2019)]{kornblith2019cka}\n398\tS.~Kornblith, M.~Norouzi, H.~Lee, and G.~Hinton.\n399\t\\newblock Similarity of neural network representations revisited.\n400\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2019.\n401\t\n402\t\\bibitem[Grant et~al.(2026)]{grant2026}\n403\tE.~Grant, et~al.\n404\t\\newblock Dissecting vision-language-action models: The visual pathway dominates robotic control.\n405\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n406\t\n407\t\\bibitem[Hou et~al.(2026)]{hou2026langgap}\n408\tY.~Hou, et~al.\n409\t\\newblock LangGap: When vision-language-action models forget to listen.\n410\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n411\t\n412\t\\bibitem[Fei et~al.(2025)]{fei2025liberoplus}\n413\tY.~Fei, et~al.\n414\t\\newblock LIBERO-Plus: Benchmarking instruction following in vision-language-action models.\n415\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2025.\n416\t\n417\t\\bibitem[Fang et~al.(2025)]{fang2025erosion}\n418\tA.~Fang, et~al.\n419\t\\newblock Fine-tuning erodes pretrained reasoning in vision-language models.\n420\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n421\t\n422\t\\end{thebibliography}\n423\t}\n424\t\n425\t\\end{document}\n426\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01YMZR2Ut5PENTD1SzPLZjDQ","session_id":"fa2e3739-cf35-4bf4-84d7-4c529d34ab0f","uuid":"9322d34f-6848-469a-a9f7-ac899e7e095b","timestamp":"2026-05-15T22:59:41.750Z"}
